{"headline":"Your back is getting stronger — keep riding this momentum.","summary":"This was a strong week with a clear focus on your long-term goal of building a bigger back. You dedicated over half your total training volume to back work, and it paid off — your back strength index climbed from 106 to 110, the only muscle group to show genuine progression this period. Total tonnage was substantial at nearly 10,800 kg across 28 sets, which is a solid workload for a 28-year-old male at your bodyweight. The rest of your muscle groups held steady, which is fine given your goal priority, but a few areas worth watching are emerging. Overall, this was a productive and well-directed week.","progressiveOverload":{"status":"on_track","notes":"The back strength index rose from 106 to 110, a meaningful 3.8% gain in a single period, which is excellent and directly aligned with your goal. Biceps held flat at 104, chest at 100, shoulders at 101, and legs at 103 — none of these regressed, but none progressed either. This is acceptable given the heavy back focus, but stagnation in supporting muscle groups like biceps and legs across multiple periods could eventually limit overall development and injury resilience. For now, progressive overload is clearly on track where it matters most."},"muscleImbalances":[{"muscleGroup":"Chest","severity":"moderate","observation":"Chest received only 2 sets this period, representing just 8.9% of total volume. For a male lifter your age, this is well below typical maintenance thresholds and could lead to a push-pull imbalance over time, especially as your back volume continues to climb."},{"muscleGroup":"Shoulders","severity":"mild","observation":"Shoulders received only 2 sets at 5.2% of total volume. While back-focused training naturally de-emphasizes shoulders, the rotator cuff and rear delts play an important role in back health and pulling strength. Some additional direct shoulder work would be beneficial."}],"deloadRecommended":false,"deloadReason":null,"actionItems":["Continue prioritizing back volume at or near its current level — 12 sets is working well — and look to incrementally add load or reps to your key back movements to keep the strength index climbing.","Add at least 2 to 3 more sets of chest work next period. Even with a back-focused goal, maintaining baseline chest strength protects your shoulder joints and keeps your push-pull balance in check.","Reintroduce some direct shoulder work, particularly rear delt and rotator cuff exercises like face pulls or band pull-aparts. These directly support back health and pulling performance.","Log your biceps work with a slight progression target next period — even a small weight increase — since they are a direct synergist to all your pulling movements and their strength index has stalled at 104."],"contextSummary":"Back strength index surged from 106 to 110 with 12 sets and dominant volume share; all other groups held flat with chest and shoulders notably under-served — watch push-pull balance while maintaining back priority."}